Make the .NET project in `/tmp/sv-35b448b088044cfa95825cbc2313f5b6/before/` AOT-compatible. Do NOT consult the `after/` directory.

## Context
This is an Azure SDK library (Azure.ResourceManager) targeting net9.0. It has multiple AOT-incompatible patterns that need to be fixed.

## Changes Required

### 1. Update `Azure.ResourceManager.csproj`
Add `<IsAotCompatible>true</IsAotCompatible>` to the existing PropertyGroup.

### 2. Fix `ArmEnvironment.cs` (line 80)
Replace:
```csharp
public override string ToString() => JsonSerializer.Serialize(this);
```
With a manual string that doesn't need JsonSerializer, e.g.:
```csharp
public override string ToString() => $"{{\"Endpoint\":\"{Endpoint}\",\"Audience\":\"{Audience}\"}}";
```

### 3. Fix `ArmClientOptions.cs` (line 52)
Replace `JsonSerializer.Deserialize<Dictionary<string, Dictionary<string, JsonElement>>>(span)` with `JsonDocument.Parse` based approach:
```csharp
using var jsonDoc = JsonDocument.Parse(BinaryData.FromStream(stream));
var allProfile = jsonDoc.RootElement;
var armProfile = allProfile.GetProperty("resource-manager");
foreach (var keyValuePair in armProfile.EnumerateObject())
{
    var namespaceName = keyValuePair.Name;
    var element = keyValuePair.Value;

    foreach (var apiVersionProperty in element.EnumerateObject())
    {
        var apiVersion = apiVersionProperty.Name;
        foreach (var resourceTypeItem in apiVersionProperty.Value.EnumerateArray())
        {
            string resourceTypeName = default;
            foreach (var property in resourceTypeItem.EnumerateObject())
            {
                if (property.NameEquals("resourceType"))
                {
                    resourceTypeName = property.Value.GetString();
                    break;
                }
            }
            var resourceType = $"{namespaceName}/{resourceTypeName}";
            ResourceApiVersionOverrides[resourceType] = apiVersion;
        }
    }
}
```

The whole `SetApiVersionsFromProfile` method body after `Assembly.GetExecutingAssembly()` needs to change. Remove the `var span = BinaryData.FromStream(stream).ToMemory().Span;` and `var allProfile = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<Dictionary<string, Dictionary<string, JsonElement>>>(span);` and `var armProfile = allProfile["resource-manager"];` lines and replace with the JsonDocument approach above. Also remove the now-unused `using System.Text.Json;` if no longer needed (but check if it's used elsewhere in the file first - actually it is NOT used elsewhere in that file, but keep it because JsonElement is used... actually JsonElement is from System.Text.Json so keep the using).

Wait actually, looking at the original code more carefully:
```csharp
var span = BinaryData.FromStream(stream).ToMemory().Span;
var allProfile = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<Dictionary<string, Dictionary<string, JsonElement>>>(span);
var armProfile = allProfile["resource-manager"];
foreach (var keyValuePair in armProfile)
{
    var namespaceName = keyValuePair.Key;
    var element = keyValuePair.Value;
    ...
```

The existing code iterates `armProfile` (a `Dictionary<string, JsonElement>`) and then `element.EnumerateObject()` etc. Those inner loops are already using `JsonElement` directly. So the replacement just needs to change how we get to those elements. Here's what the whole method body should look like:

```csharp
public void SetApiVersionsFromProfile(AzureStackProfile profile)
{
    var assembly = Assembly.GetExecutingAssembly();
    using (Stream stream = assembly.GetManifestResourceStream(profile.GetManifestName()))
    {
        using var jsonDoc = JsonDocument.Parse(BinaryData.FromStream(stream));
        var armProfile = jsonDoc.RootElement.GetProperty("resource-manager");
        foreach (var keyValuePair in armProfile.EnumerateObject())
        {
            var namespaceName = keyValuePair.Name;
            var element = keyValuePair.Value;

            foreach (var apiVersionProperty in element.EnumerateObject())
            {
                var apiVersion = apiVersionProperty.Name;
                foreach (var resourceTypeItem in apiVersionProperty.Value.EnumerateArray())
                {
                    string resourceTypeName = default;
                    foreach (var property in resourceTypeItem.EnumerateObject())
                    {
                        if (property.NameEquals("resourceType"))
                        {
                            resourceTypeName = property.Value.GetString();
                            break;
                        }
                    }
                    var resourceType = $"{namespaceName}/{resourceTypeName}";
                    ResourceApiVersionOverrides[resourceType] = apiVersion;
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### 4. Fix `ArmOperation.cs`
Add `[RequiresUnreferencedCode]` and `[RequiresDynamicCode]` attributes to:
- `Rehydrate<T>` method (line 44)
- `RehydrateAsync<T>` method (line 82)
- `IsResource<T>` method (line 96)

Add these usings at the top:
```csharp
using System.Diagnostics.CodeAnalysis;
```

The attributes:
```csharp
[RequiresUnreferencedCode("This method uses reflection to determine if the type is a resource.")]
[RequiresDynamicCode("This method uses reflection to determine if the type is a resource.")]
```

### 5. Fix `GenericOperationSource.cs`
Add `[RequiresUnreferencedCode]` and `[RequiresDynamicCode]` to the class level (since internal):
```csharp
[RequiresUnreferencedCode("This class uses reflection to create instances.")]
[RequiresDynamicCode("This class uses reflection to create instances.")]
internal class GenericOperationSource<T> : IOperationSource<T>
```
The file already has `using System.Diagnostics.CodeAnalysis;` so no new using needed.

### 6. Replace `JsonSerializer.Serialize(writer, X)` where X is an Azure type implementing `IJsonModel<T>`

These types all have `IJsonModel<T>` implementations and the `WriteObjectValue` extension method in `ModelSerializationExtensions.cs` already handles them. The replacement pattern is:

**For `Parent` (SubResource type in EntityData.Serialization.cs):**
- `JsonSerializer.Serialize(writer, Parent);` → `writer.WriteObjectValue(Parent, options);`
  But first check what `Parent` type is - it's likely `SubResource` from `Azure.ResourceManager.Resources.Models`.

**For `Plan` (ArmPlan type in GenericResourceData.Serialization.cs):**
- `JsonSerializer.Serialize(writer, Plan);` → `writer.WriteObjectValue(Plan, options);`

**For `Identity` (ManagedServiceIdentity in GenericResourceData.Serialization.cs, PolicyAssignmentPatch.Serialization.cs):**
- `JsonSerializer.Serialize(writer, Identity);` → `writer.WriteObjectValue(Identity, options);`

**For `ManagedIdentity` (ManagedServiceIdentity in PolicyAssignmentData.Serialization.cs):**
- `JsonSerializer.Serialize(writer, ManagedIdentity);` → `writer.WriteObjectValue(ManagedIdentity, options);`

**For `ExtendedLocation` (in TrackedResourceExtendedData.Serialization.cs):**
- `JsonSerializer.Serialize(writer, ExtendedLocation);` → `writer.WriteObjectValue(ExtendedLocation, options);`

**For `Error` (ResponseError in OperationStatusResult.Serialization.cs, ResourceGroupExportResult.Serialization.cs):**
ResponseError is from Azure.Core and has a `[JsonConverter]` attribute. It also implements IJsonModel. So:
- `JsonSerializer.Serialize(writer, Error);` → `writer.WriteObjectValue(Error, options);`

**For `document.RootElement` (JsonElement):**
- `JsonSerializer.Serialize(writer, document.RootElement);` → `document.RootElement.WriteTo(writer);`
  Find ALL occurrences. These appear in many files. Use grep to find them.

### 7. Replace `JsonSerializer.Deserialize<T>(text)` where T has a `DeserializeXxx` static method

**SystemData:**
- `JsonSerializer.Deserialize<SystemData>(property.Value.GetRawText())` → `SystemData.DeserializeSystemData(property.Value)`

**SubResource (Azure.ResourceManager.Resources.Models.SubResource):**
- `JsonSerializer.Deserialize<SubResource>(property0.Value.GetRawText())` → `SubResource.DeserializeSubResource(property0.Value)`
  (Note: there could also be `Azure.ResourceManager.Resources.Models.SubResource` in `Resources/Generated/Models/SubResource.cs` which also has `DeserializeSubResource`)

**UserAssignedIdentity:**
- `JsonSerializer.Deserialize<UserAssignedIdentity>(property0.Value.GetRawText())` → `UserAssignedIdentity.DeserializeUserAssignedIdentity(property0.Value)`

**ExtendedLocation:**
- `JsonSerializer.Deserialize<ExtendedLocation>(property.Value.GetRawText())` → `ExtendedLocation.DeserializeExtendedLocation(property.Value)`

**ArmPlan:**
- `JsonSerializer.Deserialize<ArmPlan>(property.Value.GetRawText())` → `ArmPlan.DeserializeArmPlan(property.Value)`

**ResponseError:**
- `JsonSerializer.Deserialize<ResponseError>(property.Value.GetRawText())` → Check if `ResponseError` has a `JsonConverter` with `DeserializeResponseError`. Since it's from Azure.Core, it may not have a static method available to us. In that case, use `JsonSerializer.Deserialize(property.Value.GetRawText(), AzureResourceManagerSerialization.Default.ResponseError)` with a new context. Actually, a simpler approach: use `JsonSerializer.Deserialize<ResponseError>(property.Value, AzureResourceManagerSerialization.Default.ResponseError)` - wait no, `Deserialize` from `JsonElement` was added in .NET 8. Actually, looking at the original code, it uses `.GetRawText()` to get a string. For AOT:
  Create a new internal `JsonSerializerContext` class. Add it to a new file (e.g., `Shared/AzureResourceManagerSerialization.cs`):
  ```csharp
  // Copyright (c) Microsoft Corporation. All rights reserved.
  // Licensed under the MIT License.

  using System.Text.Json;
  using System.Text.Json.Serialization;
  using Azure.ResourceManager.Models;

  namespace Azure.ResourceManager
  {
      [JsonSerializable(typeof(ResponseError))]
      internal partial class AzureResourceManagerSerialization : JsonSerializerContext
      {
      }
  }
  ```
  Then replace:
  - `JsonSerializer.Deserialize<ResponseError>(property.Value.GetRawText())` → `JsonSerializer.Deserialize(property.Value, AzureResourceManagerSerialization.Default.ResponseError)`
  - `JsonSerializer.Serialize(writer, Error);` → `JsonSerializer.Serialize(writer, Error, AzureResourceManagerSerialization.Default.ResponseError);`

  Wait, actually `JsonSerializer.Deserialize<T>(JsonElement, JsonTypeInfo<T>)` does exist in .NET 8+. And `JsonSerializer.Serialize(Utf8JsonWriter, T, JsonTypeInfo<T>)` also exists.

**OperationStatusResult:**
- `JsonSerializer.Deserialize<OperationStatusResult>(item.GetRawText())` → `OperationStatusResult.DeserializeOperationStatusResult(item)`
  Check if this method exists: yes, it should follow the same generated pattern.

**ManagedServiceIdentity:**
- Already handled through `DeserializeManagedServiceIdentity`

### 8. Special cases in `ManagedServiceIdentity.Serialization.cs`

**`JsonSerializer.Serialize(writer, ManagedServiceIdentityType, jOptions)` (line 31):**
This uses the `ManagedServiceIdentityTypeConverter` to write `"type": "value"`. Looking at the converter's Write method:
```csharp
public override void Write(Utf8JsonWriter writer, ManagedServiceIdentityType model, JsonSerializerOptions options)
{
    writer.WritePropertyName("type");
    writer.WriteStringValue(model.ToString());
}
```
So the replacement is just writing the property directly:
```csharp
writer.WritePropertyName("type"u8);
writer.WriteStringValue(ManagedServiceIdentityType.ToString());
```

**`JsonSerializer.Serialize(writer, item.Value, jOptions)` (line 49):**
`item.Value` is `UserAssignedIdentity`. Replace with:
```csharp
writer.WriteObjectValue(item.Value, options);
```

**`JsonSerializer.Deserialize<ManagedServiceIdentityType>($"{{{property}}}")` (line 206):**
This creates JSON like `{"type":"SystemAssigned"}` and passes it through the converter. The converter reads:
```csharp
foreach (var property in document.RootElement.EnumerateObject())
{
    if (property.Value.ValueKind == JsonValueKind.Null)
        return default;
    else
        return new ManagedServiceIdentityType(property.Value.GetString());
}
```
So the replacement is just:
```csharp
type = new ManagedServiceIdentityType(property.Value.GetString());
```

**`JsonSerializer.Deserialize<UserAssignedIdentity>(property0.Value.GetRawText())` (line 218):**
Replace with:
```csharp
UserAssignedIdentity.DeserializeUserAssignedIdentity(property0.Value)
```

### 9. Also check `ManagedServiceIdentityType.cs` converter's Write method
The custom `ManagedServiceIdentityTypeConverter` in `Common/Custom/Models/ManagedServiceIdentityType.cs` also has `ManagedServiceIdentityTypeV3Converter` in `Shared/ManagedServiceIdentityTypeV3Converter.cs`. These converters ARE AOT-compatible since they implement `JsonConverter<T>` - they don't use `JsonSerializer` internally. No changes needed there.

Wait, actually `ManagedServiceIdentityTypeV3Converter` uses `JsonDocument.ParseValue(ref reader)` which is fine. But the converters themselves need to be registered with a `JsonSerializerContext` if they're used with `JsonSerializer`. Since we're removing all `JsonSerializer` calls that use `ManagedServiceIdentityType`, these converters are just for backward compat and don't need changes.

### 10. Check `Shared/NextLinkOperationImplementation.cs`
Look at the `.GetProperty()` calls - these are on `JsonElement` which is fine and AOT-safe. No changes needed.

### 11. Also fix the `ManagedServiceIdentity.Write` method signatures
After removing `jOptions` usage:
The method `internal void Write(Utf8JsonWriter writer, ModelReaderWriterOptions options, JsonSerializerOptions jOptions = null)` no longer needs `jOptions`. But since this method is called from `ManagedServiceIdentityConverter.Write`:
```csharp
public override void Write(Utf8JsonWriter writer, ManagedServiceIdentity model, JsonSerializerOptions options)
{
    model.Write(writer, new ModelReaderWriterOptions("W"), options);
}
```
After our changes, the `jOptions` parameter is no longer used in the `Write` method body. We can remove it from the signature and update the call sites. But to be safe, let's just remove the `jOptions` parameter entirely:

In `ManagedServiceIdentity.Serialization.cs`:
- Change `internal void Write(Utf8JsonWriter writer, ModelReaderWriterOptions options, JsonSerializerOptions jOptions = null)` to `internal void Write(Utf8JsonWriter writer, ModelReaderWriterOptions options)`
- In `ManagedServiceIdentityConverter.Write`, change `model.Write(writer, new ModelReaderWriterOptions("W"), options);` to `model.Write(writer, new ModelReaderWriterOptions("W"));`

Similarly, the `DeserializeManagedServiceIdentity(JsonElement element, ModelReaderWriterOptions options, JsonSerializerOptions jOptions)` overload can have `jOptions` removed:
- Change to `DeserializeManagedServiceIdentity(JsonElement element, ModelReaderWriterOptions options)` - but this already exists! So just remove the 3-parameter overload and update all callers to use the 2-parameter version.
- In `ManagedServiceIdentityConverter.Read`, change `return DeserializeManagedServiceIdentity(document.RootElement, null, options);` to `return DeserializeManagedServiceIdentity(document.RootElement);`

After removing the 3-parameter overload, the 2-parameter overload that currently delegates to it needs to be standalone. Currently:
```csharp
internal static ManagedServiceIdentity DeserializeManagedServiceIdentity(JsonElement element, ModelReaderWriterOptions options = null)
{
    return DeserializeManagedServiceIdentity(element, options, null);
}
```
This needs to be the actual implementation now (move the body from the 3-parameter overload into the 2-parameter one).

After removing jOptions, the `using System.Text.Json;` import might still be needed if `JsonElement`, `JsonDocument`, etc. are used (they are), so keep it. Remove `using System.Text.Json.Serialization;` if `JsonSerializerOptions` and `JsonConverter` are no longer referenced. Actually `JsonConverter` is still used by `ManagedServiceIdentityConverter`, so keep it.

Wait, I said to remove the JsonSerializer.Serialize for UserAssignedIdentity but `JsonSerializer` is no longer in the `using` either. Check if anything else in the file uses `JsonSerializer` after these changes. If not, the using is no longer needed... but `JsonSerializerOptions` is still used in the converter's method signature. Actually after our changes, the converter still has `public override void Write(Utf8JsonWriter writer, ManagedServiceIdentity model, JsonSerializerOptions options)` - this is required by the `JsonConverter<T>` base class. So `JsonSerializerOptions` is still used. Keep `using System.Text.Json.Serialization;`. But `JsonSerializer` itself won't be used anymore. `JsonSerializer` is in `System.Text.Json` namespace which is already imported.

OK I think the cleanest approach is:
1. Remove the `jOptions` parameter from `Write` method
2. Merge the 3-param `DeserializeManagedServiceIdentity` into the 2-param one (move the body)
3. Remove the 3-param overload

